 last several  days of this this offensive unfold  they're having a really hard time uh  piecing together the combat power  the military force to achieve their  military objectives to secure their  supply lines and to to  really achieve an effect on this limited  front now when we say limited we should  recognize that it's still Harkey to make  alive which is 800 kilometers that is a  enormous amount of territory I was  particularly concerned about Mario Bull  and that the Russians would be able to  take that within the span of a couple  within the spread of a couple weeks they  seem to be have given up on that uh um  operation they couch it as a victory  because they've reduced uh the Ukrainian  footprint to a really small section of  the city but they haven't even managed  to do that they're looking to reposition  their forces to to achieve something and  uh that's it's unclear how far they're  gonna get but if you look at the Dyers  situation in mariopol at the moment of  people civilians and soldiers  essentially trapped in this one part of  the city how long do you think Ukrainian  forces can withstand Russia's offensive  it's interesting I think the fact is  that the Russians had assembled  somewhere in the ballpark of about 10  000 troops to take the city and uh with  this small portion of the city they're  they've now given up on that they're  withdrawing some of those forces to  achieve some of the objectives to the  North and the dunbas  um which means that they frankly they  have less forces required to hold the  city the parts of the city that they've  secured uh which means it's permeable we  this is a perennial issue you know you  have you have you don't have enough  Force to secure the whole city so  frankly the ukrainians might be able to  even break through and do some resuppies  but I have a feeling that portion of the  city could really at this point hold out  for weeks and months okay coming back to  the the point you made about resupply  for those ukrainians courses let's talk  about what they've been supplied with  and who's that's coming from if we take  first of all a look at Europe we know  that Europe has has implemented tough  sanctions on Russia that it has applied  uh military aid and also economic aid  there has however been a lot of  criticism of Germany namely on the  hesitation to send heavy weapons but  also to issue an embargo on Russian oil  and gas can you from the perspective of  Germany understand where that reluctance  is coming from there's a long kind of  pacifist streak uh or since the end of  World War II that is is ingrained in in  uh Germany and there's also kind of a um  a lot of empathy for the Russian  perspective uh that's that again carries  over from World War II based on the the  perception that Russia is the inheritor  of the Soviet Union and uh Germany  inflicted massive harm on Russia so I in  the early times in the early uh time  frame it was it was  reasonable to be empathetic but at this  point frankly  Germany's uh position is is irrational  uh it's clearly a um now a question of  whether Germany wishes to preserve the  the Western liberal order and what  wishes to uh uh support democracies  in an onslaught from authoritarianism  and to me it's now uh past the point of  you know being understanding for  Germany's reluctance it's it's the point  where it seems absurd that Germany is  not doing enough either to impose costs  on Russia for for uh for this uh War to  reduce Russia's material support its  budgetary uh income from oil and gas  trade of course there's now the  discussion about winding down oil trade  by the end of 2022 that's a long time  away and frankly I could see the the  German government reversing course if  they see any Lifeline like if there's a  glimmer of hope I could easily see this  Administration unfortunately reversing  course on that so it could end up being  an empty promise on that note also and  then on the military front I'm pretty  pretty uh sad to see the rest of uh  Europe coming together uh with the  show's support for Ukraine for democracy  for preserving the Western liberal order  for pushing back on fascism this newest  Incarnation and fascism emanating from  Russia in Germany playing the leader of  Europe  playing such a reluctant role it is uh  it is it is in opposition to to I think  the German people's will and and frankly  Germany's values uh that the government  is is now  um  stymied or uh I can't seem to piece  together a coherent strategy moving  forward if we listen to what the German  government government has said on a  Russian oil and gas embargo it has said  that it would damage the German economy  inflict such damage on the German  economy that it would no longer be able  to confront President Putin from a  position of strength A and B that it  would perhaps lose some of the broad  support that there is in the German  society as you've said yourself and that  that is a reason to avoid a complete  Russian oil and gas embarker what do you  think of that I got to tell you that's  pretty absurd and a pretty thin uh what  we call a straw man argument uh you know  in fact  Germany much uh Germany hasn't uh hasn't  absorbed hardly any of the costs right  now the costs are being absorbed by  Ukraine that's uh that's defending  German values European values defending  democracy for the whole world absorbing  an enormous cost and for the German  government to kind of sidestep its  obligations as a leader is shockingly  bad judgment the other thing that I  think we should recognize is that there  should be a shared burden if not shared  with uh with  um Ukraine it should be shared with  Europe and Eastern Europe is absorbing  enormous amount of of the shock here  they're absorbing you know millions of  refugees they're absorbing enormous  economic costs why is it that Germany  thinks that it could sidestep the cost  it is  I I I it's hard to rationalize the  perspective that the the German uh  people in the German government are not  going to absorb some of the cost for a  crisis situation that we're in a state  of a cold war between the west and and  Russia and uh we're trying to avoid a  hot War let's pick up on the  absorbing some of the there because we  saw President Biden announcing another  800 dollars  military and economic assistance for  Ukraine as you know uh that brings I  think the total that the U.S has uh  allocated for Ukraine to 3.4 billion  dollars and you say it's not enough what  more needs to be done the budgets for  for DOD are so vast and for the US  government so vast that it seems  impressive that these no these resources  coming through seem like a lot but  actually it's not it's not a significant  uh uh portion of the US budget or the  defense budget uh not at this point not  especially if you measure it against the  the yardstick of the resources poured  into Afghanistan you know we're talking  about tens of billions of dollars not  three billion dollars so that it's it's  you need to have contacts then you need  to have a frame of reference it is not  that large an amount and we're still  well shy of what I've called for uh you  know in in some of the things I've  written which is a  lend lease program  where all the equipment that Ukraine  needs to prosecute this war could be  retained uh you know on NATO territory  in the east in Poland in Romania in  Slovakia and in warehouses where Ukraine  could draw on on this equipment as  needed not you know make a request and  then let it go through bureaucratic  channels that could take weeks and then  it takes another several weeks to  transport it but literally have  everything pre-positioned there because  we as military planners understand  what's going on we could foresee the  kinds of needs that they need that  should all be there it should be ready  for the ukrainians to draw as necessary  that's what alembly's program uh you  know functional lend lease program would  look like some of the reluctance from  the West to engage more is because of a  fear of being drawn into a bigger  conflict do you understand the concerns  from NATO of the fear of a possible  nuclear war for example because you've  said that you believe Russia has no  interest in a nuclear war they have an  interest in messaging a nuclear war but  NATO's saying we don't know that for  sure that's kind of again another straw  man argument there's nothing to on the  balance when when you when we say  something like that it's a falling prey  to kind of nuclear blackmail and  following pray to kind of almost Primal  fears of uh worst case scenarios in  reality if we start to parse out the  logic of uh how something like this  could unfold it seems uh patently absurd  that the Russians that are looking to  aggrandize uh Putin is looking to  aggrandize Russia expand Russian power  is willing to in the same breath somehow  risk a nuclear Armageddon mutually  assure destruction those two don't kind  of Jive right if I could just jump in  that would mean that you believe that  President Putin is acting is a rational  actor he is a rational actor he's a  rational actor that's been incurred  urged by the West in a lot of ways he's  been a rational actor that's been in  power for 22 years and through multiple  administrations that have looked the  other way as he uh transgressed  increasingly whether that's domestically  in under Democratic movements crushing  business interests and crushing what he  thought was an oligarchy that was  involved in Russian politics it was uh  the Chechen wars were it was uh you know  inhuman brutality uh on a smaller scale  than we're seeing now but the same  template in Syria uh in uh 2014 when  they started this war against Ukraine  these are things that we should have  taken more concerted actions against but  we thought it was limited we thought  that you know this is where it ends this  is as bad it's gonna get that is a great  deal of wishful thinking about who  Vladimir Putin was and uh what how far  he's willing to go instead what we did  was we sent signals that he could get  away with it he can push further now  this was a huge enormous miscalculation  on this part he fundamentally  misunderstood who the Ukrainian people  were he misunderstood who the German  people were he misunderstood who the  American people were and that you know  we would we we had wishful thinking  about kind of what amounted to smaller  contingencies but when when uh when it  really mattered when the stakes were  high we were going to come together  and defend our values I do have to ask  you before we let you go as we mentioned  you were instrumental in well what  eventually led to president Trump's  impeachment  imagine in 2024 another Trump presidency  how could Europe and NATO prepare then  for that scenario  far-fetched to believe that Donald Trump  can return to the presidency and the way  I do this is I you know I look at the  the  the 2020 election results he lost by  seven seven million votes significant  margin after January 6th he lost tens of  millions of people that were you know  that were disgusted by him uh  nurturing an attack on the Capitol  Building since then he's continued to to  uh my cheerlead and bandwagon for the  Vladimir Putin losing Millions more so  his short his uh slice of the pie has  continued to shrink now he might be very  well the kind of the Republican nominee  but I don't see a path to victory for  him to uh uh to the general elections I  think regardless  um you know I guess in this uh hyper uh  counterfactual scenario I think  um I think as president macron is to a  certain extent indicated uh there is an  opportunity for Europe to  take a leadership role and um in terms  of uh global stability and protecting  democracies and I think I would like to  see Germany play uh you know the  partnership is unequal there too Germany  provides the economic resources to a  certain extent France provides the kind  of the the the the division I would like  to see a shared burden uh uh where both  sides contribute kind of um both to the  economic outputs but also to the to the  vision and I'd like to see Germany take  a leading role also in the direction of  Europe  not just in the financial structures  we'll leave it there thank you so much  lieutenant colonel vinman

